{"ok": true, "summary": "Root cause: dict membership (`x in d`) and lookup (`d[x]`, `dict.get`) held a RefCell borrow of the Dict while `idx.contains_key`/`get` ran Python key equality. A key's `__eq__` can re-enter the same dict (here `d.clear()`), and the nested `borrow_mut` panicked with "RefCell already borrowed".

Fix: make `Dict.idx` an `Rc<HashMap>` so lookups can take a cheap index snapshot; added `dict_contains` and `dict_get` in rt_core.rs which hash the key, clone the index Rc under a short borrow, release the borrow before the HashMap comparison can run user code, and use the existing `ver` counter to detect structural mutation during `__eq__`/`__hash__` and restart the lookup (matching CPython's restart-on-mutation behaviour). Updated `contains`, dict views, `getitem` (including dict subclasses with `__missing__`), and `dict.get` to use these helpers.

Tested: the failing test now matches CPython on both normal and forced dynamic builds; also verified nearby cases (user `__eq__` returning True after clearing, user `__hash__` clearing, `__eq__` raising, basic dict/get/defaultdict/`__missing__` operations)."}
